% M2-L10. A THIRD SHAPE: near_limit_unsat's int_lin_eq refutation (coefficients near
% Checked.limit, D-0028's magnitude sibling rather than its width one) with two spectator
% int variables in front of it, so the shape that backjumps here is linear-equality
% reasoning under checked arithmetic rather than int_ne/int_eq or Boolean clauses.
%
% x1, x2, x3 keep near_limit_unsat's exact coefficients and right-hand side: the root
% fixpoint settles without refuting anything (bounds reasoning alone cannot see the
% infeasibility) so the search has to branch and close both children at every level,
% same as that model. p and q are unconstrained 0..1 variables, declared first so
% first-fail (smaller domain: width 2 against x1/x2/x3's width 4) picks them ahead of
% x1, x2 and x3.
%
% Every conflict here is over x1/x2/x3's levels alone, so the frames owning p's and q's
% levels skip their sibling. Measured 2026-09-18:
%
%     learning off   15 nodes, 7 decisions, 0 skipped
%     learning on     7 nodes, 4 decisions, 2 skipped
%
% The learned clauses sit on thresholds strictly inside x1/x2/x3's ladders, so this is
% the NON-convertible path, like backjump_unsat.fzn and backjump_deep_unsat.fzn -- but
% via int_lin_eq's propagator rather than int_ne/int_eq's, and under the checked-
% arithmetic path near_limit_unsat exists to exercise.
%
% Widths are 0..1 (p, q) and 0..3 (x1, x2, x3): D-0028's width bound. The coefficients
% are large (as near_limit_unsat's are) but a coefficient is not a declared domain, so
% ./scripts/check_test_widths.py's bound is unaffected.
var 0..1: p :: output_var;
var 0..1: q :: output_var;
array [1..3] of int: c = [18014398509481984, 18014398509481984, 18014398509481984];
var 0..3: x1 :: output_var;
var 0..3: x2 :: output_var;
var 0..3: x3 :: output_var;
constraint int_lin_eq(c, [x1, x2, x3], 45035996273704960);
solve satisfy;
